1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,680
July, nineteen forty-four. Normandy.

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:05,880
One American rifleman
crouches in a muddy foxhole.

3
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:07,900
He is the tip of the spear.

4
00:00:08,260 --> 00:00:10,300
But alone, he would not last long.

5
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,280
Behind him stands an
army he will never meet.

6
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:16,040
So how many men did it
take to keep him fighting?

7
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:18,180
Let's play it like a game.

8
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:20,660
Your job is to keep him in the fight.

9
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:24,220
Every mission is a real
problem the army had to solve.

10
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:26,140
If one fails, he is out.

11
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:27,620
Mission one: feed him.

12
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:30,740
A rifleman digs,
carries and marches all day.

13
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:33,580
But he cannot cook in
a hole in the ground.

14
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:35,640
So the army gave him the K-ration.

15
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:39,540
Three small boxes for one day:
breakfast, dinner and supper.

16
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:42,920
Together, they held close
to three thousand calories.

17
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:46,140
For men in combat, even
that was often not enough.

18
00:00:46,160 --> 00:00:49,200
Each meal also came with four cigarettes.

19
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:52,880
The supper box even had
chewing gum and toilet paper.

20
00:00:53,340 --> 00:00:55,060
One small trick helped too.

21
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:58,900
The outer box was waxed, so many
men burned it to heat their coffee.

22
00:00:59,460 --> 00:01:02,720
But after weeks of the same
boxes, men were sick of them.

23
00:01:02,740 --> 00:01:06,580
Even the emergency chocolate
bar tasted bad on purpose.

24
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:11,080
The army asked for it to taste only
a little better than a boiled potato.

25
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:13,680
That way, nobody would eat it as a snack.

26
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:16,280
But food creates a bigger problem.

27
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:18,580
One box weighs almost nothing.

28
00:01:18,600 --> 00:01:21,220
Millions of boxes weigh thousands of tons.

29
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:24,760
Somebody has to pack them,
count them and haul them forward.

30
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:29,140
When the front was quiet, cooks
tried to send hot food up after dark.

31
00:01:29,540 --> 00:01:32,780
So the cook and the supply
man join our invisible team.

32
00:01:33,100 --> 00:01:35,000
But every box rode on a truck.

33
00:01:35,020 --> 00:01:36,820
Every truck needed fuel.

34
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:39,820
Mission two: move everything.

35
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:42,220
In August nineteen forty-four, the Allies

36
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:44,820
broke out of Normandy
and raced across France.

37
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:48,280
But their supplies were still
coming ashore in Normandy,

38
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:50,960
over the beaches and through
the port of Cherbourg.

39
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:53,220
Worse, Cherbourg was no gift.

40
00:01:53,240 --> 00:01:56,940
The Germans had wrecked its
harbor before giving it up.

41
00:01:56,960 --> 00:02:01,740
So every mile the tanks moved forward,
the supply line grew one mile longer.

42
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:04,460
The railways could not carry it.

43
00:02:04,480 --> 00:02:08,920
Allied bombers had smashed much of the
French rail network before the invasion.

44
00:02:09,320 --> 00:02:11,920
So the army built a railway out of trucks.

45
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:13,940
They called it the Red Ball Express.

46
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:17,880
Nearly six thousand trucks
ran on one-way loop roads.

47
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:19,980
Convoys rolled day and night.

48
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,680
About three out of four
drivers were Black soldiers.

49
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:26,060
In a segregated army, most Black soldiers

50
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:29,140
were placed in service
units, not combat units.

51
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,060
So many of them drove.

52
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:35,880
Most drivers were under twenty-four,
and few had driven a truck before the war.

53
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:39,620
A round trip from Cherbourg
could take fifty-four hours.

54
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:42,620
The rules said twenty-five miles per hour.

55
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:46,580
In practice, many raced alone
through the blackout at over sixty.

56
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:49,580
By mid-November, they had delivered about

57
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,160
four hundred twelve
thousand tons of supplies.

58
00:02:52,600 --> 00:02:54,520
But it still was not enough.

59
00:02:54,540 --> 00:02:57,580
On August thirtieth,
General Patton's Third Army

60
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:00,620
asked for four hundred
thousand gallons of gasoline.

61
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:03,540
It received about thirty-two thousand.

62
00:03:03,560 --> 00:03:06,340
So the tanks stopped near
the Meuse River and waited.

63
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:11,060
For a few days, empty fuel tanks
held them back more than enemy guns.

64
00:03:11,540 --> 00:03:14,360
So the truck driver
joins our invisible team.

65
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:17,320
But fuel and food only keep a body going.

66
00:03:17,340 --> 00:03:19,000
What keeps his mind going?

67
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:21,860
Mission three: morale.

68
00:03:21,880 --> 00:03:25,480
For a soldier far from home,
few things mattered more than a letter.

69
00:03:25,800 --> 00:03:27,400
But paper is heavy.

70
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:31,500
One hundred fifty thousand one-page
letters filled thirty-seven mail sacks.

71
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:34,840
They weighed more than two
thousand five hundred pounds.

72
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:36,820
So the army shrank them.

73
00:03:36,840 --> 00:03:39,780
From June nineteen forty-two, Victory Mail

74
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:42,940
letters were photographed
onto tiny rolls of film.

75
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:46,040
The same hundred fifty
thousand letters now

76
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:49,200
fit in one sack, weighing
about forty-five pounds.

77
00:03:49,740 --> 00:03:53,280
But by early nineteen forty-five,
a new problem appeared.

78
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:58,540
Millions of letters and parcels sat stuck
in warehouses in Birmingham, England.

79
00:03:58,900 --> 00:04:01,900
Many were for soldiers who
had moved or been wounded.

80
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:05,540
Others were badly addressed,
so someone had to play detective.

81
00:04:06,020 --> 00:04:08,740
So the army sent in the Six Triple Eight.

82
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:11,100
Eight hundred fifty-five Black women of

83
00:04:11,120 --> 00:04:14,460
the Women's Army Corps,
led by Major Charity Adams.

84
00:04:14,900 --> 00:04:18,900
It was the only Black women's
battalion sent overseas in the war.

85
00:04:19,260 --> 00:04:21,560
They were told the job
would take six months.

86
00:04:21,940 --> 00:04:26,760
They worked in three shifts, seven days
a week, in cold and badly lit buildings.

87
00:04:27,200 --> 00:04:30,880
Each shift handled around
sixty-five thousand pieces of mail.

88
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:33,540
They cleared the backlog in three months.

89
00:04:33,560 --> 00:04:35,880
Their motto was short:
No mail, low morale.

90
00:04:36,320 --> 00:04:39,120
So the mail clerk joins
our invisible team.

91
00:04:39,520 --> 00:04:42,220
But some letters came
back unopened, because

92
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:44,720
the man they were
written for had been hit.

93
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:48,260
Mission four: keep him
alive when he is wounded.

94
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:52,180
Every minute counts, and here
the invisible army moves fastest.

95
00:04:52,720 --> 00:04:56,080
First comes the company medic,
with bandages and morphine.

96
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:58,600
But he cannot operate in a hedgerow.

97
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:02,960
So litter bearers carry the man
back to a battalion aid station,

98
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:05,540
often about a kilometer behind the line.

99
00:05:05,680 --> 00:05:08,740
The goal was treatment
within about an hour.

100
00:05:08,760 --> 00:05:10,860
From there, ambulances take him to

101
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:13,600
a clearing station,
where surgeons can operate.

102
00:05:14,120 --> 00:05:16,800
The worst cases go on to bigger hospitals.

103
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:20,440
Every step needs people: doctors, nurses,

104
00:05:20,460 --> 00:05:22,860
drivers, and clerks who
track every patient.

105
00:05:22,880 --> 00:05:26,120
Add blood plasma and new
drugs like penicillin.

106
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:29,380
In the First World War,
about eight of every

107
00:05:29,400 --> 00:05:32,100
hundred wounded Americans
died of their wounds.

108
00:05:32,480 --> 00:05:35,200
In Europe in this war,
it was closer to four.

109
00:05:35,680 --> 00:05:37,960
So the medic joins our invisible team.

110
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:40,380
But medicine could not save everyone.

111
00:05:40,400 --> 00:05:42,360
Mission five is the hardest.

112
00:05:42,820 --> 00:05:45,140
Sometimes a rifleman did not come back.

113
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:47,840
Even then, the army still had work to do.

114
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:52,700
Graves Registration units searched the
battlefield for every fallen soldier.

115
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:55,240
Each man wore two identity tags.

116
00:05:55,260 --> 00:05:56,520
One stayed with him.

117
00:05:56,540 --> 00:06:00,280
The other went to the records,
so his family would be told.

118
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:05,120
After the war, families got a choice:
bring him home, or let him rest overseas.

119
00:06:05,680 --> 00:06:09,100
More than one hundred seventy-one
thousand were brought home.

120
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:10,360
Others stayed.

121
00:06:10,380 --> 00:06:12,380
Today, nine thousand
three hundred eighty-eight

122
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:15,980
Americans rest in the
cemetery above Omaha Beach.

123
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:21,280
So the graves clerk joins our invisible
team, making sure no one is forgotten.

124
00:06:21,680 --> 00:06:23,880
But the front was running out of men.

125
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:26,540
Mission six: replace him.

126
00:06:26,560 --> 00:06:29,360
No matter how well he was
fed, supplied and healed,

127
00:06:29,380 --> 00:06:32,300
riflemen were lost
faster than anyone else.

128
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:35,860
By December nineteen forty-four,
commanders in Europe

129
00:06:35,880 --> 00:06:39,340
expected to be short about
twenty-three thousand riflemen.

130
00:06:39,860 --> 00:06:41,940
The army had plenty of soldiers.

131
00:06:42,300 --> 00:06:44,280
It did not have enough infantrymen.

132
00:06:44,300 --> 00:06:46,140
So it turned to its own tail.

133
00:06:46,540 --> 00:06:50,180
In September and October alone,
over fourteen thousand

134
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:54,040
replacements trained for other
jobs were retrained as riflemen.

135
00:06:54,620 --> 00:06:57,360
But in December, the Germans
struck in the Ardennes.

136
00:06:57,700 --> 00:07:00,360
In the chaos of the Battle
of the Bulge, cooks,

137
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:03,520
clerks and drivers grabbed
rifles to hold the line.

138
00:07:03,960 --> 00:07:06,240
So the army in Europe tried something new.

139
00:07:06,260 --> 00:07:10,960
It asked Black soldiers in service
units to volunteer as riflemen.

140
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:12,920
Thousands stepped forward.

141
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:16,400
Many sergeants gave up their stripes,
just for the chance to fight.

142
00:07:16,900 --> 00:07:20,880
By February, four thousand five
hundred sixty-two had volunteered.

143
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:25,060
Many fought as extra platoons
inside white rifle companies.

144
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:28,160
It was an early crack
in a segregated army.

145
00:07:28,580 --> 00:07:31,000
So the retrained rifleman joins our team.

146
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:34,540
He was never meant for the front,
until the front needed him.

147
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:37,000
But what about the enemy's own tail?

148
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:40,280
Mission seven: look at the other side.

149
00:07:40,300 --> 00:07:44,240
In newsreels, the German army looked
like a machine of tanks and trucks.

150
00:07:44,700 --> 00:07:47,360
But most German divisions moved on foot.

151
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:50,660
Their guns and supplies
were often pulled by horses.

152
00:07:51,260 --> 00:07:54,780
It started the war with about
five hundred thousand horses.

153
00:07:55,160 --> 00:07:57,960
Over the whole war,
the German army used roughly

154
00:07:57,980 --> 00:08:00,740
two and three quarter
million horses and mules.

155
00:08:01,500 --> 00:08:04,540
Horses need food, rest
and vets of their own.

156
00:08:04,800 --> 00:08:08,300
So the German supply line
had a hungry, tired tail too.

157
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:10,980
Both sides learned the same hard truth.

158
00:08:11,500 --> 00:08:15,300
A soldier at the front is only
as strong as the line behind him.

159
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:17,340
So now we can answer the question.

160
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:20,840
How many men did it take to
keep one rifleman fighting?

161
00:08:21,680 --> 00:08:25,040
Look at First Army, the American
army that landed in Normandy.

162
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:29,720
By D-Day, only about fourteen
percent of First Army was infantry.

163
00:08:30,380 --> 00:08:32,340
Just eight percent served in tanks.

164
00:08:32,360 --> 00:08:35,160
Almost forty-five percent
were support troops:

165
00:08:35,680 --> 00:08:37,860
supply, maintenance, ordnance and medical.

166
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:42,520
The rest fired the artillery, built
bridges and strung telephone wire.

167
00:08:43,060 --> 00:08:44,060
Now do the math.

168
00:08:44,080 --> 00:08:47,940
For every infantryman in that army,
there were about six other soldiers.

169
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:50,800
Six men he would mostly never meet.

170
00:08:51,280 --> 00:08:55,120
Some of them were on our team: the cook,
the driver, the mail clerk, the medic.

171
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:59,400
Others fixed engines, cleared
roads and kept the radios working.

172
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:01,220
But here is the twist.

173
00:09:01,240 --> 00:09:04,180
That small slice at the
front paid the biggest price.

174
00:09:04,680 --> 00:09:07,880
Of all the American ground
soldiers killed in battle

175
00:09:07,900 --> 00:09:11,780
in World War Two, about three
out of four were infantrymen.

176
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:13,980
So the answer has two sides.

177
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:16,620
In First Army, it took about seven

178
00:09:16,640 --> 00:09:19,100
soldiers to put one
infantryman in the line.

179
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:23,080
Yet that one man carried most
of the danger for all seven.

180
00:09:23,420 --> 00:09:25,340
Remember the tanks waiting at the Meuse?

181
00:09:25,360 --> 00:09:28,860
When the line behind broke,
even the strongest front stopped.

182
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:30,980
Battles were won at the front.

183
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:35,060
But wars were won by the long,
invisible line behind it.

184
00:09:35,440 --> 00:09:38,440
But that line had a breaking
point: the replacements.

185
00:09:38,460 --> 00:09:40,560
Next time, find out what happened to

186
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:43,020
a teenager who reached
the front knowing nobody.

187
00:09:43,840 --> 00:09:45,740
Subscribe so you don't miss that story.
